body:
No terrestrial laboratory is exactly inertial: it rotates with Earth and is subject to gravity, vibration and other disturbances. We can nevertheless treat it as inertial when those effects are negligible over the region and duration of the experiment, at the precision we need.
For example, a train coasting along a straight track can provide a useful approximation. Once it brakes or rounds a bend, however, the same inertial description no longer applies throughout its motion. A spacecraft coasting far from large masses can be a better approximation. In each case we must assess the physical disturbances; a smooth-looking coordinate plot is not enough.
\optional_details{Why acceleration is different}{Uniform relative motion cannot be detected by experiments confined to one ideal inertial frame. Acceleration is different: an accelerometer can detect it locally, without a view of an outside reference object. A pendulum or the apparent curvature of otherwise free-particle tracks can also reveal that a laboratory frame is not inertial.}

